BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260808T180000Z
DTEND:20260808T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20261004T164459Z
UID:c4rmaopo6ph68bb569gjcb9k60r3ab9o61j3ab9ocopjip9hcopjae1n6s@google.com
CREATED:20260804T232522Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Miles's goal:</b><br><span>To learn\, understanding and do t
 he positions well enough to be able to self-critique and increase arrow cou
 nt and strength</span><br><b><br></b><br><b>What we worked on:</b><br>1. Re
 visiting the positions and correct sequencing from STANCE through SET  UP.<
 br><ul><li>STANCE - Good</li><li>NOCK - GOOD</li><li>HOOK - Little finger a
 nd thumb need to be in position before the bow is raised</li><li>GRIP - Che
 ck the hand position before raising the bow\, making sure full pressure on 
 base of thumb with no gap between that and the riser.</li><li>SET (and main
 tain positions until end of the shot)<ul><li>Posture - knees locked\, quads
 /glutes tight\, core tight\, chest down\, shoulder relaxed up\, down\, and 
 back and level\, head turned left as full-face to target as possible</li><l
 i>Set arrow line - draw elbow - wrist a bit out\, not in - to end of arrow.
 </li><li>Reduce tension in shoulders</li></ul></li><li>SET UP - Biggest cha
 llenge - needs lots of work!<ul><li>Raise the bow to the height of your nos
 e (or higher if you need to use gravity to help you while you building your
  strength) - <ul><li>watch that shoulders stay relaxed\, down and back in l
 ine with each other and don't raise when you raise the bow </li></ul></li><
 li>Come down with both arms\, as you turn your torso to line up your two sh
 oulders with your bow arm<ul><li>Bow arm stays slightly higher than draw ar
 m\, so you never move the sight up into the target</li><li>Watch that draw 
 arm elbow does not drop below shoulder - should be at arrow height</li><li>
 Draw hand comes down and in and finishes between front shoulder and neck at
  shoulder height before starting DRAW to LOAD</li><li>Draw wrist does not b
 end towards neck\, but rather stays as the outermost point in the alignment
 .</li><li>Draw elbow should finish behind and in line with the string when 
 viewed from the elbow view. A few inches to go. Will be solved by more coil
 ing from the top of SET UP to the bottom of SET UP.</li></ul></li></ul></li
 ></ul><br>2. Understanding and doing DRAW to LOAD<br><ul><li>DRAW to LOAD<u
 l><li>Focus should be on moving the LAN 2 parallel to the shooting line\, c
 ompressing the midback - shoulder blade will move more towards spine. </li>
 <li>Angular motion - draw elbow moves around in an arc at shoulder height w
 hile forearm moves diagonally towards the neck</li><li>At LOAD\, string sho
 uld be touching the front right edge of your chin</li><li>Minimal gap betwe
 en draw hand and neck</li></ul></li></ul><br>3. Considering draw weights<br
 ><ul><li>Competition bow is at the moment too heavy at almost 19#<ul><li>Pr
 actice raising the bow and pulling back the string incrementally:<ul><li>to
  SET - and then break down</li><li>to SET UP - and then break down</li><li>
 to DRAW to LOAD - and then break down</li></ul></li><li>Ideally\, use the 1
 7# range bow when in the indoor classes until your form and strength can ha
 ndle your competition bow.</li></ul></li></ul><br>4. Way forward in terms o
 f what to practice and how frequently<br><ul><li>Every other day starting M
 on 8/10.</li><li>Video yourself and look at your positions in the videos af
 ter you do each one constantly so you get the relationship between what you
  feel and what is actually happening. </li><li>Send me one video per sessio
 n to keep you accountable. I'll briefly comment and hope to see weekly impr
 ovements.</li><li>Start with SET/ breakdown\, and repeat until you are fati
 gued. Take 80% and practice that every other day. So\, for example\, if you
  manage to do 30 reps of SET/breakdown before you are fatigued\, then do .8
 0 x 30 reps = 24 SET/breakdowns every other day for 4 sessions to really so
 lidify the position.</li><li>After 4 sessions\, add SET UP/breakdown\, find
 ing first the max reps and then taking 80% of the reps for the next 4 sessi
 ons.</li><li>Send me a video each practice day.</li><li>After I see these t
 wo positions are ingrained\, I'll let you know that you can go on to DRAW t
 o LOAD or we can have another private session to work on it \, before pract
 icing it at home.</li></ul>
LAST-MODIFIED:20260808T230856Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Private Lesson with Coach Racae - Miles
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
